{"name":"Pedestrian Green","hex":"#00bb22","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pedestrian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or intended for those who are walking.","Ordinary, dull; everyday; unexceptional."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A walker; one who walks or goes on foot, especially as opposed to one who uses a vehicle.","An expert or professional walker or runner; one who performs feats of walking or running."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pedestrian"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pedestrian","blocks":[{"text":"A pedestrian is a person traveling on foot, by wheelchair or with other mobility aids. Streets and roads often have a designated footpath for pedestrian traffic, called the sidewalk in North American English, the pavement in British English, and the footpath in Australian, Indian and New Zealand English. There are also footpaths not associated with thoroughfares; these include rural paths and urban short cuts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/CrossWalk_%285465840138%29.jpg/330px-CrossWalk_%285465840138%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedestrian","wikidata":"Q221488"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Pedestre.JPG/500px-Pedestre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Pedestre.JPG/1280px-Pedestre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Pedestre.JPG/500px-Pedestre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A sign in Belo Horizonte, Brazil, directing pedestrians to an overpass for safe crossing.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APedestre.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Colecci%C3%B3n_de_hombres_cruzando.JPG/500px-Colecci%C3%B3n_de_hombres_cruzando.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Colecci%C3%B3n_de_hombres_cruzando.JPG/1280px-Colecci%C3%B3n_de_hombres_cruzando.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2